.TH WK 1 "2026-10-22" "wk" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wk \- manage Git worktrees and their development sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wk
.RI [ --herdr | --tmux | --backend " backend" ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wk
manages a project arranged as one bare Git repository with one linked worktree
per branch.  It starts and manages a Herdr workspace by default, or a tmux
session when configured or requested.
.PP
Run commands from the project root or from any worktree.  The project root
contains a
.I .wk.toml
file and normally a
.I .bare
Git directory.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI add " branch-name [badge]"
Create a worktree for a new branch based on the configured base ref.  If
.I origin/branch-name
exists, check it out and track it instead.
.TP
.BI add " origin/branch-name [badge]"
Create a worktree with a local branch named
.I branch-name
that tracks the specified remote branch.
.PP
An optional badge prefixes the Herdr workspace label with
.BR [badge] ,
leaving the configured label (including its project suffix) after it.
Supplying a badge also updates an existing workspace's label.  Omitting it
leaves an existing label unchanged.  Branch names and worktree paths do not
change.  Badges are stored only in Herdr's label; supply the badge again when
recreating a closed workspace.  Badges require the Herdr backend.
.PP
Setup runs in the launching pane; keep that pane open, but you may switch to
another pane, tab, or workspace while it runs.  Herdr workspace creation uses
the explicit project root as its source, rather than the focused pane.
Inside Herdr, add focuses the destination only if the launching workspace,
tab, and pane are still focused when it finishes.  If you switch elsewhere,
it leaves focus unchanged and reports that the workspace is ready.  Missing
caller identity or an unavailable or incomplete focus snapshot also prevents
automatic switching.  Attaching from outside Herdr and tmux behavior are
unchanged.
.TP
.BI delete " worktree-dir"
Remove a worktree and its session, delete its branch, and prune stale
worktree metadata.
.I rm
is an alias.  The argument may be a worktree directory, branch name, or
.B .
for the current worktree.
.TP
.B clean
Offer to remove worktrees whose pull request has merged.  This command uses
.BR gh (1)
to look up pull-request state.
.TP
.B ls
Show the project's worktrees and session status.
.TP
.B ls --porcelain
Print parse-friendly three-line blocks: branch, directory, and session.
.TP
.B init
Create a starter
.I .wk.toml
in the current directory.
.TP
.BI completion " zsh|bash|fish"
Print the shell completion script for the selected shell.
.TP
.BR version , " -v" , " --version"
Print the wk version.
.TP
.BR help , " -h" , " --help"
Print command help.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --herdr
Use the Herdr session backend for this invocation.  This is the default unless
configured otherwise.
.TP
.B --tmux
Use the tmux session backend for this invocation.
.TP
.BI --backend " herdr|tmux"
Equivalent to the matching short backend option.  Backend options may appear
before or after the command; if more than one is supplied, the last one wins.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .wk.toml
Per-project configuration.  Its optional fields are
.BR name ,
.BR base_ref ,
.BR setup ,
and
.BR teardown .
.TP
.I ~/.config/wk/config.toml
Global configuration.  The supported fields are
.BR default_base_ref ,
.BR session_template ,
and
.BR session_backend .
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.B wk
requires Git.  It requires Herdr for the default backend or tmux when that
backend is selected.  The
.B clean
command additionally needs the GitHub CLI,
.BR gh (1).
Commands in a project's
.B setup
or
.B teardown
configuration are run by
.BR sh (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B wk
exits with status 0 on success and non-zero if a command fails or its arguments
are invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR gh (1)
